- op: discuss  # c491
  at: '2026-07-31T17:53:45Z'  # c491
  by: agent  # c491
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c491
  clock: 3  # c491
  nonce: c491  # c491
  message: "PREMISE RE-MEASURED (L1). The symptom is real; the mechanic is wrong, and the\nproposed fix would have been built in the wrong module.\n\nWRONG: \"The matcher implementation enumerates 8 supported match surfaces, all of\nwhich key off declared decorators, inheritance, parameter types, or name\npatterns.\" There is a NINTH surface. framework_patterns.UsagePatternSpec matches\nUsageContext records on (kind, name, position), where kind is one of\ncall / data_value / export / macro. Its own docstring: \"Usage patterns match\nagainst UsageContext records emitted by analyzers for CALL-BASED frameworks like\nDjango, Express, Go Gin.\" ir.UsageContext says the same: \"This enables YAML\npattern matching for call-based frameworks (Django, Express, Go) where route\nhandlers are registered via function calls rather than decorators.\"\n\nIt is not new, either: UsagePatternSpec traces to 55296f5440 (2026-01-30), about\nfour months BEFORE this item was filed.\n\nWRONG: \"A new framework integration for such a library is structurally\nunwritable.\" A YAML author can write `usage: {kind: call, name: ..., position:\nargs[1]}` today; that is exactly how django.yaml keys route handlers.\n\nMEASURED, with a positive control (L9 — a null is only evidence once a control\nproves the measurement can produce a positive):\n\n  django urls (POSITIVE CONTROL)    symbols=4  usage_contexts=1\n      kind='call'  name='path'  position='args[1]'\n  fastapi decorator routes          symbols=3  usage_contexts=0\n  app=FastAPI(); Flask(__name__);\n  Base=declarative_base()           symbols=4  usage_contexts=0\n\n(First probe returned 0 for the control too; the probe was wrong, not the code —\nanalyze_python takes a REPO ROOT, not a file path. L10.)\n\nTHE ACTUAL DEFECT IS IN THE PRODUCER, NOT THE MATCHER. py.py emits UsageContext\nonly for an enumerated allowlist of framework call names:\n\n    py.py:473   DJANGO_URL_FUNCTIONS = {\"path\", \"re_path\", \"url\"}\n    py.py:931   if func_name not in DJANGO_URL_FUNCTIONS: continue\n    py.py:1320  if func_name not in FLASK_URL_FUNCTIONS: continue\n\nSo the matcher's call surface exists and works, and there is simply nothing for a\nconstructor-keyed pattern to match: no analyzer emits a UsageContext for\n`app = FastAPI()`. A YAML author writing the pattern gets silence — which is the\nsame user-visible outcome the item describes, arrived at by a different route,\nand repaired in a different file.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE FIX. The item's remedy (\"Match surfaces include\ncall/instantiates/constructor_call\") would add matcher capability that already\nexists and still produce zero matches. The real options are:\n\n  (a) Emit UsageContext for constructor/factory calls in the producers — nine\n      analyzers emit UsageContext today (clojure, elixir, go, js_ts, php, py,\n      ruby, rust, swift), each with its own hardcoded call-name allowlist, so\n      this is an N-producer problem and wants a chokepoint, not nine edits.\n  (b) Better lead, and it is the item's OWN observation: the substrate already\n      carries ~7,939 `instantiates` edges. Those are EDGES, and the matcher\n      consumes UsageContexts, so \"the matcher simply does not consume it\" is\n      right about the data and wrong about why. Matching patterns against\n      existing edges would need no new producer emission at all.\n\nRe-title before working: this is not \"the framework matcher lacks entry\nfamilies.\" Suggested: \"UsageContext is emitted only for enumerated framework\ncall names, so constructor/factory-keyed YAML patterns can never match.\"\n\nPriority unchanged (P1) — the consequence stands: a whole class of frameworks is\ninvisible to YAML pattern matching with no error or warning."  # c491
- op: discuss  # b699
  at: '2026-07-31T18:11:23Z'  # b699
  by: agent  # b699
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b699
  clock: 4  # b699
  nonce: b699  # b699
  message: "SECOND PASS — against ADR-3ccc, the governing document. This SUPERSEDES the\n\"producer allowlist\" reading in my previous note, which was too generous to the\n\"just emit more UsageContexts\" fix. Both remedies I floated fail as stated.\n\nADR-3ccc (Status: Implemented) is the ADR that added call-based framework\nmatching. Its model, stated precisely:\n\n    A FIRST-PARTY SYMBOL APPEARS SOMEWHERE INSIDE A FRAMEWORK CALL / DATA /\n    EXPORT SITE.\n\nThat is why UsageContext.symbol_ref is documented as \"ID of the symbol being\nused\", and why every `position` value the ADR enumerates and every one the 22\nshipped framework YAMLs use is an INPUT position:\n\n    args[1] · args[-1] · args[0].handler · :get (map key) · default · block ·\n    export name\n\nWI-nopod'S CASES INVERT THAT MODEL. In `app = FastAPI()` the framework-revealing\nthing is the CALLEE and the symbol we want to tag is the call's RESULT. There is\nno input position for it because it is not an input. ADR-3ccc's own \"Survey of\nFramework Patterns\" enumerates decorator / call / data-driven / file-based /\nblock-DSL and has NO constructor-or-factory row. This is a FIFTH PATTERN\nCATEGORY the ADR never surveyed, not a gap inside the fourth.\n\nTHE \"JUST CONSUME THE instantiates EDGES\" LEAD IS WORSE THAN IT LOOKS. Measured\non `app = FastAPI(); flask_app = Flask(__name__); Base = declarative_base()`:\n\n    instantiates   file:main.py -> external_symbol:FastAPI   (evidence ast_new)\n    instantiates   file:main.py -> external_symbol:Flask     (evidence ast_new)\n    calls          file:main.py -> external_symbol:declarative_base\n    contains       file:main.py -> variable:app\n    contains       file:main.py -> variable:flask_app\n    contains       file:main.py -> variable:Base\n\nand the `app` node in full:\n\n    kind=variable  name='app'  signature=None  qualified_name=None\n    modifiers=[]   meta={'visibility_signal': 'default'}\n\nThe `instantiates` edge is anchored at the FILE, and `app` carries NOTHING tying\nit to FastAPI. So \"the substrate already contains 7,939 instantiates edges, the\nmatcher simply does not consume it\" is right that the edges exist and wrong about\nwhat they say: they record THAT THIS FILE INSTANTIATED FastAPI SOMEWHERE, never\nTHAT `app` IS A FastAPI. Consuming them can tag the file; it can never tag the\nvariable, which is the thing every one of the item's examples wants tagged.\n\nNote this is a MISSING fact, not a mis-anchored one — at module scope the file is\nthe correct enclosing scope for the call. What is absent is the BINDING:\n`app` <- result of `FastAPI()`. No edge, no meta, no signature carries it.\n(Distinguish from WI-pimun, where io_edge_src IS mis-anchored to a file when an\nenclosing callable exists. Related shape, different defect — do not fold them.)\n\nSO THE REAL PRECONDITION IS: emit the binding. Until `app` is connected to\n`FastAPI` by something, neither matching path is available, and any YAML a\nframework author writes matches nothing.\n\nFIX SHAPE, PER R1/R2 AND THE THROUGH-LINE. Before minting a new IR type or a new\nedge type, ask whether the fact already has a home (R1: seven instances of that\nreversal). It arguably does — a UsageContext with a RESULT position, e.g.\n`kind=\"call\"`, `context_name=\"FastAPI\"`, `symbol_ref=<app>`, `position=\"result\"`.\n`position` is free-form and regex-matched, so this needs no schema change, and it\nconsolidates onto the existing record instead of adding a peer. Nine analyzers\nemit UsageContext, each with its own hardcoded call-name allowlist (py.py:473\nDJANGO_URL_FUNCTIONS = {\"path\",\"re_path\",\"url\"}; FLASK_URL_FUNCTIONS at 1320), so\nthe emission itself is an N-producer problem and wants a chokepoint, not nine\nedits — and a parity gate before any of them, per the WI-duguk methodology.\n\nTWO THINGS THIS OPENS THAT I AM NOT DECIDING UNILATERALLY:\n\n  1. It needs an ADR-3ccc amendment. Adding a fifth pattern category to an ADR\n     whose Status is \"Implemented\" is an ADR-grade change (docs/, not a\n     governance file, but not a drive-by either).\n  2. It stretches UsageContext's own semantics. symbol_ref is documented as \"the\n     symbol being used\"; an assignment target is PRODUCED by the call, not used\n     at it. That is a live concept question on a field that already carries a\n     documented axis-mixing note and a completed audit (WI-gatuh-ruvar, done;\n     WI-hofoj documented the triggers). It does not fire trigger (a) — no fifth\n     `kind` value — but it is exactly the kind of stretch the triggers exist to\n     catch. CORRECTION to my previous note and to what I told the owner: I said\n     WI-nopod was independent of any concept question. It is independent of the\n     TYPE-KIND audit; it is not concept-free.\n\nBOTTOM LINE ON SIZE. This is not \"re-title and pick a remedy.\" It is: rule on the\nUsageContext semantics stretch, amend ADR-3ccc, build a parity gate, then a\nchokepoint + N producers. The item's P1 severity is unchanged and its consequence\nstands, but it is a multi-session shape, not a quick win."  # b699
