- op: discuss  # 50cc
  at: '2026-08-01T01:36:26Z'  # 50cc
  by: agent  # 50cc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 50cc
  clock: 10  # 50cc
  nonce: 50cc  # 50cc
  message: "Re-titled. The old title — \"Framework matcher lacks call/instantiates/constructor_call entry families\" — named a defect that does not exist: framework_patterns.UsagePatternSpec has matched UsageContext on (kind, name, position) since 55296f5440 (2026-01-30), four months before this item was filed, and its own docstring says it is for \"call-based frameworks like Django, Express, Go Gin\". The real gap was always in the PRODUCERS, and the new title says so. It also now scopes to what remains after PR #137: Python records meta[\"constructed_from\"], eight analyzers do not. Consequence and P1 severity unchanged. NOTE: fields.statement, fields.root_cause and the original description still carry the superseded mechanic — left intact as the filing record rather than rewritten, with the two measurement notes above as the correction. Flagging per the standing lesson that a stale statement is what readers trust for scope while corrections live in discussion."  # 50cc
- op: update  # d096
  at: '2026-08-01T01:38:18Z'  # d096
  by: agent  # d096
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d096
  clock: 11  # d096
  nonce: d096  # d096
  set:  # d096
    description: "**Mechanic.** A whole class of frameworks is configured by *constructing* an\nobject rather than by decorating or subclassing one — `app = FastAPI()`,\n`app = Flask(__name__)`, `Base = declarative_base()`, `runner = CliRunner()`.\nNothing recorded that binding, so no YAML pattern could key on it.\n\nThe matcher was never the problem. `framework_patterns.UsagePatternSpec` has\nmatched `UsageContext` on `(kind, name, position)` since `55296f5440`\n(2026-01-30), and its own docstring says it exists \"for call-based frameworks\nlike Django, Express, Go Gin\". What was missing sat one layer down, in two\nplaces:\n\n1. **ADR-3ccc's model is directional.** It captures *\"a first-party symbol\n   appears **inside** a framework call\"* — `UsageContext.symbol_ref` is \"the\n   symbol being used\", and every `position` the ADR defines is an input slot\n   (`args[1]`, `args[0].handler`, a map key, `default`, `block`). These cases\n   invert it: the symbol to tag is the call's **result**. ADR-3ccc's own\n   \"Survey of Framework Patterns\" has no constructor/factory row.\n2. **The producers emit `UsageContext` only for enumerated framework call\n   names** (`py.py:473 DJANGO_URL_FUNCTIONS = {\"path\",\"re_path\",\"url\"}`,\n   `FLASK_URL_FUNCTIONS` at 1320). A constructor-keyed pattern would match\n   nothing even where the surface existed.\n\n**Expectation.** A framework-integration author can key a YAML pattern to a\nconstruction site for any language whose analyzer records the binding.\n\n**Current state (PR #137).** Python records it as\n`Symbol.meta[\"constructed_from\"]` — the callee, qualification intact\n(`fastapi.FastAPI`, not `FastAPI`) — registered on the `symbol_meta` axis.\nThe matcher needed **no** change: `Pattern.meta_match` already takes a\n`{key: regex}` map over `Symbol.meta`, asserted by\n`test_meta_match_selects_a_constructed_object`. Deliberately narrow: only a\ncall result is stamped; factory and constructor are treated identically\n(static analysis cannot distinguish them); a computed callee\n(`factories[key]()`) records nothing rather than a partial name.\n\n**What remains — the producer sweep, unblocked but not started.**\n* Eight analyzers emit `UsageContext` and none stamps `constructed_from`\n  (clojure, elixir, go, js_ts, php, ruby, rust, swift). Each carries its own\n  hardcoded call-name allowlist, so this wants a chokepoint, not eight edits.\n* **No framework YAML keys on this yet.** The capability is untested against\n  a real integration; writing one (FastAPI or SQLAlchemy `declarative_base`)\n  is the cheapest end-to-end proof and should come *before* the sweep.\n* A parity column over `constructed_from` per language would make the gaps\n  visible, on the evidence of the interface-dispatch column finding five\n  causes nobody was looking for.\n\n**Trap for whoever writes that YAML:** `meta_match` applies `regex.match`,\nnot `search`. `(^|\\.)FastAPI$` silently fails on `fastapi.FastAPI`;\n`(.*\\.)?FastAPI$` works. Pinned with a tripwire in\n`test_constructed_from_matching.py`.\n\n**Exposure.** Any YAML author writing an integration for a call-based library\nin the eight unswept languages; any consumer of a substrate produced from a\ncodebase using such a library.\n\n**Consequence.** For those languages the framework family stays structurally\ninvisible to YAML-driven matching, with no error or warning — the only path\nis a hand-stitched downstream linker, reproducing the exact silent-failure\nmode the YAML system was meant to abstract.\n\n---\n\n### As originally filed (2026-06-05, `dogfood_tranche_03`) — superseded\n\nPreserved verbatim; its mechanic is wrong and its two proposed remedies both\nfail, but the symptom and severity it reported were real and it is the reason\nthis was found.\n\n> **Mechanic**: The matcher implementation enumerates 8 supported match\n> surfaces, all of which key off declared decorators, inheritance, parameter\n> types, or name patterns. The substrate already contains 7,939\n> `instantiates` edges and 619 `dispatches_to` edges, so the underlying graph\n> data is present; the matcher simply does not consume it. Libraries that\n> expose their framework surface as constructor calls (`app = FastAPI()`,\n> `app = Flask(__name__)`, `Base = declarative_base()`, `runner = CliRunner()`)\n> or as factory calls have no key a YAML author can use. A new framework\n> integration for such a library is structurally unwritable.\n>\n> **Expectation**: Match surfaces include call/instantiates/constructor_call\n> so framework authors can key patterns to construction sites; OR the docs\n> explicitly mark the call-based framework family as out of scope and\n> consumers know they must hand-stitch downstream linkers.\n>\n> **Scope**: All frameworks whose primary surface is call/instantiation:\n> argparse, fire, Flask/FastAPI app construction, SQLAlchemy\n> declarative_base, gRPC service stubs, anything Pydantic, anything\n> metaclass-driven.\n>\n> **Source**: F47.A3 (INDEPENDENT, HIGH). Substrate sha256 a32c4a31...,\n> HEAD 9a18959071.\n\n**Why it was wrong, measured:** the matcher's call surface predates the\nfiling by four months; and the ~7,939 `instantiates` edges are anchored at\nthe **file** (*\"this file instantiated FastAPI somewhere\"*) while the bound\nvariable carried no link to the callee at all — so consuming them could tag\nthe file, never the variable every example wants tagged."  # d096
