  message: "DONE. The item's own scope is complete: a framework author can now key a YAML\npattern to a construction site, and the first two integrations that do so are\nshipped.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE — the chain end to end, not a proxy. Re-run on dev\n(1a0780834d), full pipeline over a repo declaring fastapi + flask:\n\n    app         constructed_from='FastAPI'    concepts=['application']\n    wsgi        constructed_from='Flask'      concepts=['application']\n    api_router  constructed_from='APIRouter'  concepts=['router']\n    bp          constructed_from='Blueprint'  concepts=['router']\n    plain       constructed_from=None         concepts=[]\n\nPinned by `test_shipped_yaml_tags_constructed_apps_end_to_end`. Independent\ncorroboration: `docs/CONCEPTS.md` regenerates with fastapi and flask listed\nunder both `application` and `router`, which is derived from the YAML corpus\nrather than from anything I asserted.\n\nTHREE PHASES, in the order chosen because each de-risked the next.\n\n1. A real framework YAML FIRST, before any sweep. Cheapest proof the whole\n   chain works, and it immediately caught a defect no review would have: the\n   patterns were appended to the end of fastapi.yaml, which put them inside\n   the `linkers:` list rather than `patterns:`. yamllint passed, the file\n   parsed, and `load_framework_patterns` returned ZERO of them. Now guarded\n   by `test_the_shipped_patterns_are_under_patterns_not_linkers`.\n\n2. A parity column BEFORE the sweep, which changed the plan. \"Eight analyzers\n   need the key\" was the stated scope; measurement found TWO hole shapes:\n\n     KEY MISSING (6) js, ts, go, swift, rust, clojure  -> all closed\n     NO SYMBOL   (3) ruby, php, elixir                 -> WI-kavam\n\n   The second group emits no module-level variable at all, so there is\n   nothing to stamp — a prerequisite gap of a different size. Bundling them\n   would have buried three real analyzer defects inside a mechanical sweep\n   and reported \"eight done\" when six were.\n\n3. The sweep through ONE shared helper (`analyze.base.constructed_from_callee`)\n   rather than six copies, since a fact recorded in N places is the defect\n   this whole campaign kept finding. Clojure's s-expression form is the one\n   genuine exception. The helper falls back to a leading name-shaped child\n   when a grammar names no callee field, which is how Swift works with no\n   special case.\n\nWHAT DID NOT SURVIVE CONTACT, worth recording because both were mine:\n  * The expected callee cannot be shared across languages. Rust's idiom is an\n    associated function (`C::new`), Go's a constructor function (`NewC`), JS\n    `new C()`. A single expectation quietly mis-passed Rust until it was split\n    per-language.\n  * Go's `value` FIELD resolves to the `expression_list` wrapper rather than\n    being absent. Assuming absence is why Go silently recorded nothing on the\n    first attempt — the helper now unwraps whichever it is handed.\n\nREMAINING, TRACKED ELSEWHERE — not this item:\n  * WI-kavam — ruby / php / elixir emit no module-level variable symbol.\n    Each language's parity cell flips from strict-xfail to hard pass on fix.\n  * INV-kinin — gained a new selection route measured here: a test that drives\n    an analyzer through the CLI is linked by the import graph to `cli.py`, not\n    to the analyzer, so it is selected or not depending on what ELSE is in the\n    diff. `BRANCHES_test_swift.py` was the sole coverer of swift's\n    protocol-requirement branch and was absent from the manifest. Worked\n    around with a direct-import test; the class is open.\n\nNOT CLAIMED: no bakeoff has run against this, and no real-world corpus has\nbeen measured for how many construction sites the six languages now surface.\nThe capability is proven on fixtures and on two framework integrations; the\nvolume is unmeasured."  # d39e
